s it.","magnitude":null,"share_of_change":null},{"code":"methodology_revision","title":"FY2019 figure revised between editions","basis":"official","evidence":"FY2019 Weighted civil filings per judgeship: 381 (JB-2019 to JFF-2022) \u2192 332 (JFF-2023). The tables' notes don't explain the change.","magnitude":-49.0,"share_of_change":null},{"code":"methodology_revision","title":"FY2020 figure revised between editions","basis":"official","evidence":"FY2020 Weighted civil filings per judgeship: 554 (JB-2020 to JFF-2022) \u2192 322 (JFF-2023 to JFF-2025). The tables' notes don't explain the change.","magnitude":-232.0,"share_of_change":null},{"code":"methodology_revision","title":"Inference: N.D. Fla. accounts for most of the FY2020 revision","basis":"inferred","evidence":"In JB-2020 Table X-1A, N.D. Fla.'s filings contribute 211 points to the national 554; the later edition's figure is 232 points lower. That is consistent with the revised series excluding or down-weighting those cases (mostly directly filed MDL cases), but no published note says so. Unconfirmed: needs AO confirmation.","magnitude":-210.57962852897472,"share_of_change":0.907670812624891}]},"clarification":null,"declined":null,"provenance":[{"source":"official_table","table_code":"X-1A","series":"Judicial Business of the United States Courts","edition":"JB-2019","period":{"fiscal_year":2019,"period_end":"2019-09-30","period_type":"fiscal_year"},"definition":"Civil filings multiplied by Federal Judicial Center case weights (which reflect the relative judge time each case type needs), divided by authorized judgeships. The Judicial Conference uses weighted filings when assessing judgeship needs.","url":"https://www.uscourts.gov/sites/default/files/data_tables/jb_x1a_0930.2019.xlsx","cell_ref":"Table X-1A.!G6","revision_status":"revised","first_published":{"value":381.0,"edition":"JB-2019","table_code":"X-1A","url":"https://www.uscourts.gov/sites/default/files/data_tables/jb_x1a_0930.2019.xlsx","cell_ref":"Table X-1A.!G6"},"later_revisions":[{"value":332.0,"edition":"JFF-2023","table_code":"6.2","url":"https://www.uscourts.gov/sites/default/files/data_tables/jff_6.2_0930.2023.xlsx","cell_ref":"Table 6.2!D9"}],"reconciliation":null,"dataset_release":null,"note":null},{"source":"official_table","table_code":"X-1A","series":"Judicial Business of the United States Courts","edition":"JB-2020","period":{"fiscal_year":2020,"period_end":"2020-09-30","period_type":"fiscal_year"},"definition":"Civil filings multiplied by Federal Judicial Center case weights (which reflect the relative judge time each case type needs), divided by authorized judgeships. The Judicial Conference uses weighted filings when assessing judgeship needs.","url":"https://www.uscourts.gov/sites/default/files/data_tables/jb_x1a_0930.2020.xlsx","cell_ref":"Table X-1A.!G6","revision_status":"revised","first_published":{"value":554.0,"edition":"JB-2020","table_code":"X-1A","url":"https://www.uscourts.gov/sites/default/files/data_tables/jb_x1a_0930.2020.xlsx","cell_ref":"Table X-1A.!G6"},"later_revisions":[{"value":322.0,"edition":"JFF-2023","table_code":"6.2","url":"https://www.uscourts.gov/sites/default/files/data_tables/jff_6.2_0930.2023.xlsx","cell_ref":"Table 6.2!D8"}],"reconciliation":null,"dataset_release":null,"note":null}],"warnings":[{"code":"inferred_not_official","message":"The explanation of the FY2020 revision is our inference from the district figures, not an official statement.","severity":"caution"},{"code":"revised_figure","message":"The breakdown uses JB-2019: 381 and JB-2020: 554, the first-published series (the only one with district rows). The current official figures are 332 (JFF-2023) and 322 (JFF-2025).","severity":"caution"}],"suggested_next":[]},"error":null,"latency_ms":651.6}

